# Line endings, for the files where they are not cosmetic.
#
# Git's default on Windows is core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites every text
# file in the working tree to CRLF on checkout. For source that is harmless.
# For these it is not, and each one fails in a way that looks like something
# else entirely — which is why they are named here rather than left to whoever
# next clones this on Windows to work out.

# The guest image build runs under a POSIX shell, on Linux or in WSL. A CRLF
# script is "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M"; a CRLF alpine.pins compares every
# checksum against itself plus a carriage return and reports that the mirror is
# serving something else.
*.sh            text eol=lf
sandbox/guest/init      text eol=lf
sandbox/guest/alpine.pins text eol=lf

# The golden terminal frames are compared byte for byte against what the
# renderer produced, and the renderer ends its lines with LF on every platform.
*.frame         -text

# The pinned Alpine packages. Binary, whatever core.autocrlf says.
*.apk           binary
